# Highlighting for `.kite` files.
#
# GitHub highlights through Linguist, which only knows languages submitted to
# it, and there is no Kite entry yet — the submission is written and waiting in
# `packaging/linguist/` in the language's own repository.
#
# This names Kite regardless. Linguist ignores an override it cannot resolve,
# so until the entry lands the line does nothing and `.kite` goes uncoloured
# here. The alternative — `linguist-language=Rust` — does colour these files,
# by asserting in the one file a repository keeps for stating what its sources
# are that `app/src/` is Rust. It is not, and the resemblance that makes the
# mapping tempting is exactly what makes the claim misread: `var`, `check` and
# `nil` are Kite, and a reader told this is Rust reads them as mistakes. The
# day Linguist accepts the entry this line starts working, with no edit.
#
# `linguist-detectable` makes `.kite` files count in this repository's language
# statistics — dormant until then, for the same reason.
*.kite linguist-language=Kite
*.kite linguist-detectable

# `npm run fmt:check` compares `kitec fmt` output against the file byte for
# byte. Without this git hands Windows a CRLF checkout and every one of them
# "fails" for a reason that has nothing to do with layout.
*.kite text eol=lf
